--TEST--
Core\Storage::list filters on a `prefix` that is empty or itself an object key, and refuses one no key on the disk could begin with — ADR 0082 § 2, `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `prefix` is the row's one option — optional, so ADR 0063 R3 puts it in the
// single trailing shape rather than in a second positional slot — and it is
// bounded on both sides. This case names the last accepted spelling and the
// first refused one together, because a member that stopped one entry early
// would print plausibly against either half alone.
//
// The accepted side. Empty is the default and is every object on the disk; a
// prefix that is a key is the objects whose keys begin with it.
echo "all: ", Core\Str::join(Core\Storage::list("uploads"), ","), "\n";
echo "empty: ", Core\Str::join(Core\Storage::list("uploads", {prefix: ""}), ","), "\n";
echo "note: ", Core\Str::join(Core\Storage::list("uploads", {prefix: "note"}), ","), "\n";
echo "note.: ", Core\Str::join(Core\Storage::list("uploads", {prefix: "note."}), ","), "\n";

// A prefix that happens to match nothing is a different question from one that
// could never match anything: this one is a key, so it is asked and answers an
// empty array.
echo "zz: ", Core\Arr::count(Core\Storage::list("uploads", {prefix: "zz"})), "\n";

// The refused side. Every non-empty prefix of a key is itself a key, so a
// spelling the key grammar turns down is one no key on this disk can begin
// with — and `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused` refuses it rather than repairing it into an empty
// answer. The message is the key grammar's own, because that is exactly what
// is wrong with it.
try {
    Core\Storage::list("uploads", {prefix: "note/"});
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
try {
    Core\Storage::list("uploads", {prefix: ".no"});
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[storage.uploads]
root = "./objects"

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./objects"]
write = ["./objects"]
--FILE objects/note.txt--
one
--FILE objects/note.md--
two
--FILE objects/notebook--
three: a key needs no extension
--FILE objects/other.txt--
four: the one `note` does not reach
--EXPECT--
all: note.md,note.txt,notebook,other.txt
empty: note.md,note.txt,notebook,other.txt
note: note.md,note.txt,notebook
note.: note.md,note.txt
zz: 0
Core\Storage::list: `note/` is not an object key — one to 255 bytes of ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`, not beginning with a `.`. A key names an object on the disk and never a path through it, so a separator is refused rather than resolved
Core\Storage::list: `.no` is not an object key — one to 255 bytes of ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`, not beginning with a `.`. A key names an object on the disk and never a path through it, so a separator is refused rather than resolved
